-- Lekiu payroll: EPF is charged from KWSP's Third Schedule bands, not as a rate
-- of wages (issue #423, part 1).
--
-- The rules table could already name a payer, a wage band and a maximum, so the
-- only thing missing was the schedule's own figure. A rate is not that figure: a
-- band pays one amount for every wage inside it, so 11% of RM1,000.01 is 110.00
-- where the Schedule prints 113.00, and 12% of RM5,000.01 is 600.00 where the
-- employer owes 612.00. Measured on this migration's parent, against every whole
-- ringgit in every band, the flat rate was wrong on 2,904 of 3,208 employee
-- points and 4,231 of 4,812 employer points; the worst employee shortfall was
-- RM11.00 at RM5,000.01 (550.00 against 561.00) and the worst overpayment RM1.10
-- in the first band, which the Schedule makes NIL up to RM10.00.
--
-- Where the figures come from: KWSP's Third Schedule from 1 October 2025, the
-- document the issue links. It is 8 pages of real text and does not load in a
-- browser from here (403 on kwsp.gov.my, a Cloudflare page in headless Chrome),
-- so it came through a text extraction service as three conversions of the same
-- schedule. Each conversion drops the one row that straddles a page break, so the
-- bands are the union of the three: 401 rows in each of Parts A, C and E from
-- RM0.01 to RM20,000.00, two RM10 bands, 249 in RM20 steps and 150 in RM100
-- steps, 0 gaps, 0 arithmetic mismatches, and the 1,145 rows held by more than
-- one conversion agree in every case. Part B and Part D are deleted by Act
-- A1760/2025. Part F is a rate, not a band, and stays a row of its own.
--
-- Proved against KWSP's own worked examples rather than against a rate: RM3,250
-- is Part A 424.00 / 359.00 / 783.00, Part C 212.00 / 180.00 / 392.00 and Part E
-- 131.00 / 0.00 / 131.00 in the schedule this migration loads, and
-- supabase/tests/people_payroll_epf_bands_check.sql compares the function's own
-- answer with those figures and with the band edges.
--
-- What changes for a caller. The key does not move: (scheme, payer, citizenship,
-- employee_from_age, wage_from_cents) still names one row, and the EPF rate rows
-- that remain (above RM20,000 and Part F) sit at their own wage_from_cents. The
-- proposal reads a band's amount when the row has one and falls back to the rate
-- otherwise, so SOCSO and EIS, which stay rates here, answer exactly as before.
-- A citizen below 60 has no employee row from 60, so nothing pays an employee
-- share past that age (Part E is the employer's 4%).
--
-- Still open in #423: the PERKESO banded tables and the EIS exemption at 57.

-- ---- the column a band's figure lives in --------------------------------
-- rate keeps its range and loses only its NOT NULL: a band row has no rate, and a
-- row that carried a meaningless one would be read as a rate by anything that
-- looked at the column instead of the amount.
alter table public.hr_statutory_rules
  alter column rate drop not null,
  add column amount_cents bigint check (amount_cents is null or amount_cents >= 0);

-- The rate rows this migration replaces. Every EPF row is rewritten below, so the
-- delete takes all of them, not only the ones a band supersedes.
delete from public.hr_statutory_rules where scheme = 'epf';

